 inclusionary rule until 2019)","name":"minneapolis hiawatha 2008 2012","note":"no permit data with unit counts","parking":"unknown","per_far":null,"per_far_high":null,"per_far_low":null,"role":"context","role_label":"Background only"}],"city_checks":[{"doc":{"label":"Net-effect results","url":"https://github.com/mroconnell/housing-challenge-starter-missingmiddleware/blob/main/docs/tod/NET_EFFECT_RESULTS.md"},"text":"In 8 US cities that upzoned land near transit, we measured the extra homes permitted over five years, net of any building pulled from elsewhere in the city, as a share of all the city's homes. It ranged from 0.04% (Los Angeles) to 1.24% (Madison); Saint Paul, with one year of data before its reform, was -0.12%. In no city did building fall on the land next to the upzoned area. These whole-city shares are small because each reform covered a small slice of its city.","title":"Our own cities' whole-city gains"},{"doc":{"label":"Control-city results","url":"https://github.com/mroconnell/housing-challenge-starter-missingmiddleware/blob/main/docs/tod/CONTROL_CITIES_RESULTS.md"},"text":"We also compared each reform city's total building with 10 similar US cities that made no such reform, over the same years (6 cities; New York and Philadelphia rezoned in many steps, so they could not be compared this way). Whole-city building swings so much from city to city that this comparison can only detect a change of about 4% to 5% of a city's homes over five years, far more than the reforms added. 5 of the 6 cities' own measurements fall inside its likely range, but it is too noisy to narrow Wisconsin's range.","title":"Matched cities with no transit reform"}],"classification":[{"city":"Charlotte","data":"A","mandate":"no","parking":"yes","strength":"strong","use":"Measured: used to place homes","year":"2019"},{"city":"New York","data":"A","mandate":"no","parking":"no","strength":"strong","use":"Measured: used to place homes","year":"2004"},{"city":"New York (Brooklyn)"